%% Compute shaders on slides: WebGPU programs that run live and step with the talk.
%%   lmr2svg examples/compute/deck.lmr -o compute.html
%% Needs a browser with WebGPU (current Chrome, Edge, Safari; Firefox on Windows).
!title Thinking in Parallel
!madewith
!subtitle compute shaders on slides, running live on your GPU
!theme dark

!slide A parallel sum in five steps

!columns[42 58]
	!column
		32 threads add 32 numbers. After each level they meet at a
		**barrier**; half of them add a neighbour's partial sum.

		:: wgsl[1-2|4-6|4-6|4-6|4-6|4-6|9]
			s[i] = vals[i];
			workgroupBarrier();
			for (var d = 1u; d < N; d *= 2u) {
			  if (i % (2u * d) == 0u) {
			    s[i] += s[i + d];
			  }
			  workgroupBarrier();
			}
			if (i == 0u) { total = s[0]; }

		Sequentially: 31 additions. In parallel: $\log_2 32 = 5$ rounds.
	!column
		!compute
			!src reduce.wgsl
			!steps slide

!slide Atomics: a million threads, 256 bins

Each thread draws one sample, the sum of $k$ uniform numbers, and adds it to a
histogram. Many threads hit the same bin at once, so the count is an
`atomicAdd`. As $k$ grows, the histogram becomes the normal curve:

!compute
	!src clt.wgsl
	!steps 4

!slide Reaction and diffusion

!columns[40 60]
	!column[.center]
		Two chemicals diffuse and react:

		:: math
			\partial_t u = D_u \nabla^2 u - u v^2 + F (1 - u) \\
			\partial_t v = D_v \nabla^2 v + u v^2 - (F + k)\, v

		Every cell is a thread; each keypress moves the feed $F$ and kill
		rate $k$: spots, coral, mazes, holes.
	!column
		!compute
			!src grayscott.wgsl
			!steps 3
			!rate 300

!slide[.plain]

!compute
	!src flow.wgsl
	!viewport full
	!steps 2

!gap[fill]

!style[.center]
	[A quarter of a million particles in a curl-noise flow: press → for a vortex, then two.]{#dfe8f2}

!slide Put a compute shader on your slide

!columns[45 55]
	!column
		:: lemur
			!compute
				!src sim.wgsl
				!steps 3

		:: wgsl
			@group(0) @binding(1) var<storage,
			  read_write> cells: array<f32, 65536>;

			//! threads 65536
			@compute @workgroup_size(64)
			fn tick(@builtin(global_invocation_id)
			        id: vec3u) {
			  cells[id.x] += lmr.dt;
			}

			fn mainImage(p: vec2f) -> vec4f { … }
	!column
		!table
		!cols Provided | Meaning
		!noheader

			`lmr.step`, `lmr.step_raw` | the step: eased, and whole
			`lmr.time`, `lmr.dt`, `lmr.tick` | simulation time, timestep, tick
			`lmr.resolution`, `lmr.mouse` | the canvas, the pointer
			`lmr.seed`, `lmr_rand(x)` | a seed, a hash to $[0, 1)$
			`//! threads N` | the threads a kernel runs
			`//! once` | a kernel that initialises

		Going back restores the simulation; jumping ahead replays it.
